  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "A parabola has a turning point located at $(1,2)$. Its $x$-intercepts are $(-1,0)$ and $(3,0)$. Find the equation of the parabola in:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p14-q4-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "turning point form",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=-\\frac12(x-1)^2+2$",
          "worked": "The turning point gives $y=A(x-1)^2+2$. Substitute $(-1,0)$:\n$$\\begin{align*}0&=A(-1-1)^2+2\\\\0&=4A+2\\\\A&=-\\frac12\\\\y&=-\\frac12(x-1)^2+2.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q4-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "intercept form",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=-\\frac12(x+1)(x-3)$",
          "worked": "The intercepts give $y=A(x+1)(x-3)$. Substitute the turning point $(1,2)$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2&=A(1+1)(1-3)\\\\2&=-4A\\\\A&=-\\frac12\\\\y&=-\\frac12(x+1)(x-3).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      }
    ]
  },
